WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:06.799
Over a decade of Marvel movie magic, 21 films
in the MCU, dozens of Easter eggs, and hundreds

00:00:06.799 --> 00:00:11.890
of minutes of action and emotion — it has
all led up to this moment: the release of

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:13.120
Avengers: Endgame.

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:17.820
Ahead of Endgame's theatrical debut, reviews
for the superhero ensemble started hitting

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:18.420
the internet.

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.540
Critics near and far raced to let their thoughts
about Endgame be heard — and you'll want

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:23.580
to lend them your ear.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.179
Here's what the critics on Rotten Tomatoes
are saying about Avengers: Endgame.

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:29.940
Don't worry: you're entering a spoiler-free
zone!

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.359
There's a lot riding on Avengers: Endgame
to be a worthy successor to Avengers: Infinity

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:38.820
War and a capstone to three phases of the
MCU so far.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:41.800
Thankfully, Endgame seems to have doubly succeeded.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:46.400
Critics say it pays off everything within
itself and much that preceded it: risks of

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:51.900
past movies, bets the heroes make here, the
lengthy run time, the stakes and storylines,

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:53.800
the plot we won't spoil — everything.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.760
Mashable's Angie Han wrote:

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.000
"The bet pays off.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.300
The story feels more organic this time, the
emotions more earned, and the interpersonal

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.340
dynamics richer and more lived-in."

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:09.100
Den of Geek called the film "a totally triumphant
payoff," while Susana Polo of Polygon said

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.680
"there's nothing Endgame sets up that it doesn't
pay off."

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:13.740
Said David Sims of The Atlantic:

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:18.420
"The film earns its length not by overstuffing
the frame with opulent action, but by slowing

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:21.460
things down and basking in the charisma of
its ensemble.

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.280
[...] Thanos's demand for a dramatic ending
in Infinity War pays off here in all the right

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:26.780
ways."

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:31.950
Russ Fischer at Birth.Movies.Death. agreed:
"Rather than closing things down, Endgame

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:33.100
opens everything up.

00:01:33.329 --> 00:01:37.680
This is a genre film with layers of overlapping
Big Ideas, character conflicts that could

00:01:37.689 --> 00:01:42.470
be flat-out ridiculous if handled with less
skill, and deep in-jokes featuring dozens

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:46.780
of costumed characters […] Ultimately, Endgame
is an entire movie of payoffs.

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:51.460
It plays off and completes thoughts begun
in Age of Ultron and Civil War, and even in

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:52.220
comics."

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:57.140
If you thought Avengers: Infinity War was
heartbreaking, you apparently have a big storm

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:58.600
coming with Avengers: Endgame.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:03.200
Critics are advising fans to pack some hankies
and wear waterproof mascara when they head

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.020
out to see the film, as it's packed with tear-jerking
emotions of all sorts.

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:11.620
Hey, it even reportedly made Captain America
himself, Chris Evans, cry six times.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:15.140
INSIDER's Kirsten Acuna wants moviegoers to
hit the theater prepared:

00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:17.720
"Bust out the tissues, Marvel fans.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:22.560
Avengers: Endgame is going to make you laugh,
cheer aloud, tear up multiple times, and cry.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:26.840
By the film's end, there were audible sniffles
throughout INSIDER's 10 AM press screening

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:27.680
Tuesday morning.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:30.520
Endgame is an emotional punch straight to
the gut.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:34.820
[...] You're going to be too emotionally distraught
by the film's ending to really nitpick at

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:36.080
anything in Endgame."

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:39.460
Viewers won't weep exclusively out of sadness,
though.

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:43.560
As Rosie Fletcher and Richard Jordan at Den
of Geek wrote in their joint review:

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.460
"At times Endgame is heartbreaking — expect
to cry.

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:50.960
But there are certain scenes that border on
the euphoric; certain character moments, surprises

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:55.540
and reveals that hit so hard in the heart
and the head that it feels like you're MCU-drunk."

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:59.940
Even Richard Roeper of the Chicago Sun-Times
couldn't help but be moved by Endgame:

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:03.870
"It's a serious contender for the crown and
it's the undisputed champion when it comes

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:04.840
to emotional punch.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:09.560
[...] Avengers: Endgame is as emotionally
affecting as any Marvel movie has ever been."

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:14.740
Okay, so we know the movie will probably make
you cry – but that doesn't mean the movie

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:16.860
is a sob-fest from start to finish.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.040
Critics have indicated that Endgame features
plenty of the MCU's trademark humor, which

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:22.950
helps steady the sentimentality.

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:25.180
Jake Kleinman of Inverse explained:

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:29.939
"Thor delivers some of the best jokes in Endgame
(sometimes without saying a word)."

00:03:29.939 --> 00:03:32.790
The Washington Post's Michael O'Sullivan concurred,
saying,

00:03:32.790 --> 00:03:36.640
"As high as the stakes are in Endgame, it
is also a very funny movie."

00:03:36.909 --> 00:03:40.760
It seems one of the movie's most prominent
strong points is its ability to mix emotion

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.660
with comedy — and avoid tipping too far
to one side.

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:45.920
As The Guardian's Peter Bradshaw wrote,

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:51.159
"The sheer enjoyment and fun that it delivers,
the pure exotic spectacle, are irresistible,

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:54.620
as is its insouciant way of combining the
serious and the comic.

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.120
Without the comedy, the drama would not be
palatable.

00:03:57.260 --> 00:04:01.510
Yet without the earnest, almost childlike
belief in the seriousness of what is at stake,

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:02.840
the funny stuff would not work either."

00:04:03.470 --> 00:04:07.640
National Review critic Kyle Smith might have
said it best when he wrote, "[A] somber aura

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:12.920
gives Avengers: Endgame its gravitas, but
the movie is also funny, rousing, and, above

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:13.920
all, endearing."

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:18.680
Where there are lovers, there are haters — or,
in this case, people who didn't fall completely

00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:20.180
head over heels for the feature.

00:04:20.660 --> 00:04:25.100
Bold and beautiful as it evidently is, the
film still isn't immune from critique.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:29.920
Though the vast majority of reviewers basically
gave Endgame full marks, a few critics took

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.520
a more, well, critical approach to the film.

00:04:33.290 --> 00:04:37.220
Forbes senior contributor Scott Mendelson
wrote that Endgame is "flawed" and feels as

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:41.530
if it "copied" certain beats of Infinity War,
though it's still entertaining and ties up

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:43.160
the threads of the MCU nicely:

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:47.920
"It doesn't make a ton of internal sense,
suffers from narrative claustrophobia, and

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.620
the action leaves a little to be desired.

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:54.900
[...] Avengers: Endgame is a merely okay MCU
movie … [but] there is grace in its failings."

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:59.980
Donald Clarke of The Irish Times argued that
Endgame "spends too long wallowing in its

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:02.120
own pomposity" and is "short on fun."

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:06.780
Christian Long at Glide Magazine called it
a "subversive, compelling, and thoroughly

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:11.420
unsatisfying" film that "chooses to rely on
vacuous fan service instead of taking the

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:13.960
time to put any real heart into its storytelling."

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:18.080
One certified rotten review came from Globe
and Mail's Barry Hertz, who wrote, "The entire

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:22.360
endeavour loses any sense of emotional stakes
or general meaning beyond the deliverance

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:24.880
of crass fan-service and incomprehensible
visuals."

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:25.380
"Ouch."

00:05:26.260 --> 00:05:30.400
It seems that for every fault one could find
in Avengers: Endgame, ten times the amount

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:31.500
of merits exist.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:35.960
Sure, a handful of critics weren't roused
by the superhero ensemble — and that's A-okay.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:40.960
But far more were moved enough to exalt it
as a true achievement and stamp it with perfect

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:42.360
or near-perfect scores.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:43.500
Why?

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:46.660
According to Germain Lussier with io9, the
film is

00:05:46.780 --> 00:05:50.820
"everything you've ever dreamed a Marvel movie
could be" — and then some.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.420
Inverse's Jake Kleinman wrote that the film
definitely delivers on its promises of big

00:05:55.421 --> 00:06:00.250
action and bigger heart, with its third act
feeling, quote, "unlike anything we've seen

00:06:00.250 --> 00:06:02.160
before" in the history of the MCU:

00:06:02.470 --> 00:06:06.820
"As the movie finally ended, I felt different,
almost as if I was glowing like Brie Larson's

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:07.420
Captain Marvel.

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:12.120
The result of 11 years of world-building has
arrived, and it feels like nothing will be

00:06:12.120 --> 00:06:13.020
the same again."

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:17.740
In his review of Endgame, Nick Johnston at
Vanyaland said that the film is everything

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:21.310
a fan could want, and feels like an actual
ending to the story, writing,

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:25.460
"It doesn’t feel like a teaser for another
movie later on down the line [...] it’s

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:28.260
content with satisfying you, the devoted,
in the here and now."

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:32.920
The Salt Lake Tribune's Sean P. Means offered
a similar analysis, writing,

00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:37.030
"Avengers: Endgame delivers everything Marvel
fans will enjoy and not much that they expect

00:06:37.030 --> 00:06:41.360
[…] Earth's mightiest heroes fulfill their
promise, in an installment that takes a decade

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:46.340
of superhero storytelling and ties it together
into an astounding, emotional conclusion."

